+
+
+# Design analysis for the combo variant
+# -------------------------------------
+# a) Concrete behavior and trace:
+#    - Input remains the original family: 4x4 shared-color frames surround monochrome 2x2 blocks,
+#      and 1-4 structural border gaps seed outward rays.
+#    - Original task: the output preserves the frame and source block, then overlays the emitted rays.
+#    - D13 alone: simulate exactly the same rays, then erase the source frame/block from the output.
+#    - D12 alone: return only the ray layer on a blank background canvas.
+#    - Combined rule: after emission the source object is consumed, and the final raster keeps only
+#      ray pixels. In a concrete example, a red block with one NE gap still emits the same red beam
+#      and participates in the same collisions, but the green frame and red 2x2 seed vanish entirely.
+#      The gap cell remains colored because it is the first ray pixel, not part of the erased source.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    - The beam physics, collision taxonomy, gap combinatorics, block-count range, and color choices
+#      stay unchanged, so the task does not collapse into a simple one-line extrapolation problem.
+#    - The visual difference is still strong: each source removes 12-15 non-background pixels from
+#      the output while preserving only the dynamically generated trajectories.
+# c) Implicit-assumption audit:
+#    - The base solver assumed `out = g.copy()` because static sources survived. That assumption no
+#      longer holds; `out` must start as a pure background canvas.
+#    - Detection of frames, colors, and gaps must continue to read `g`, because the input still
+#      contains intact sources and the solver must infer emission from them.
+#    - Motion legality must also keep reading `g`, not `out`; otherwise erased frames would stop
+#      behaving as obstacles and the combo would accidentally alter beam physics in addition to output.
+#    - Beam seeds are still required to be background in the input, so spawning onto a blank `out`
+#      is safe and keeps the first visible pixel at the gap.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    - Generator keeps the original 4x4 frame footprint, in-bounds placement margin, and frame-frame
+#      clearance, so frames are recoverable and rays have unobstructed launch corridors.
+#    - Gap combos are still filtered against a full-frame obstacle grid so initial rays never launch
+#      directly into another source object.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    - Across a few ARC examples, a human can see that every output deletes all framed sources yet
+#      preserves the color and geometry of the emitted ray system. This is a visible structural rule,
+#      not a hidden numeric convention.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    - The output is cleaner than the base task because the static source layer is absent. Only the
+#      ray system remains, and collisions are already the semantic focus of the original puzzle.
+# g) Change inferability:
+#    - No new color remapping is introduced. Source color still equals ray color, so the learner only
+#      has to infer selective retention/erasure, not a fresh color-translation table.
+# h) Variant metric used after implementation:
+#    - `overlap_nonbg = count((output != bg) & (input != bg))` must be zero.
+#    - `erased_source_pixels = count((input != bg) & (output == bg))` must equal the count of all
+#      non-background input source pixels. Together these quantify the fused "source consumed, rays only"
+#      behavior and distinguish it from the base task.
+# i) New solver assumptions guaranteed by generation:
+#    - At least one structural gap exists, so at least one beam is spawned.
+#    - Each gap has at least `MIN_RAY_STEPS` runway through background.
+#    - Every 2x2 block is uniquely framed by a single non-background border color.
